---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/yotpo-email-sms-vs-attentive';
const title = 'Yotpo Email & SMS to Attentive for Shopify: Migration Decision';
const desc = "Yotpo Email & SMS to Attentive for Shopify: migration availability, one-time subscriber transfer, loyalty integration and retail signup checks.";

const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is this still a choice between two active senders?",
    "answer": "No. Yotpo announced Email and SMS retirement, and its current SMS notice confirms decommissioning. Attentive is a replacement candidate named in the migration announcement."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does Attentive support email as well as SMS?",
    "answer": "Yes. Its Yotpo migration guide covers both email and SMS subscriptions. Assess the email program explicitly rather than assuming Attentive is only a text sender."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is the Yotpo transfer an ongoing audience sync?",
    "answer": "The documented migration is a one-time transfer. Ask Attentive which recovery route remains available for your account now that the Yotpo dashboard is decommissioned."
  },
  {
    "question": "Do dynamic audiences migrate as live logic?",
    "answer": "Attentive says static lists become static segments while dynamic lists need rebuilding. Preserve the original rules and reconcile destination membership."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I keep Yotpo Loyalty?",
    "answer": "Attentive documents a Yotpo-built Loyalty integration. Its migration guide requires rebuilding journeys and segments with the new data and notes that old assets are not automatically removed."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I add Sequenzy for email?",
    "answer": "Evaluate the complete Attentive email and SMS proposal first. Consider Sequenzy or another sender when a specific email requirement remains unmet, with separate ownership for collection, suppression and post-purchase messages."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-cyan-100/90">/</span>
        <span class="text-cyan-100/90">Yotpo Email & SMS vs Attentive</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="badge">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Yotpo Email & SMS vs Attentive for Shopify</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-cyan-100">Yotpo Email & SMS is retired, and Attentive is one of the migration partners named by Yotpo. The decision now is whether its email and SMS migration path fits your retained records, review and loyalty setup, and subscriber collection points. A partner announcement does not establish account access or guarantee a completed migration.</p>
      </header>

      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Retirement changes the comparison</h2>
        <p>Yotpo scheduled full Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025. Its SMS end-of-life page now says the dashboard and background services are inactive. Attentive and Omnisend were named migration partners. That is a useful starting point for support discussions, not evidence that every old account still has a working transfer.</p>
        <h2>Ask which migration path still exists</h2>
        <p>Attentive published a Shopify-based Yotpo migration guide in 2025. Before changing integrations, ask its migration team which steps remain available for your store and what source records they can recover. Inventory exports and configuration records already held by the merchant. Missing subscriptions or suppression evidence should remain marked missing, rather than reconstructed from telephone numbers in orders.</p>
        <h2>Treat the transfer as a boundary</h2>
        <p>The guide describes a one-time subscriber transfer and warns that later changes in the old system do not continue syncing. Agree the authoritative collection and opt-out source before activation. Reconcile late changes around the transfer boundary and retain an exception list for unresolved records. Do not interpret a transferred contact count as proof that every recipient is eligible for both channels.</p>
        <h2>Rebuild live audiences deliberately</h2>
        <p>Static lists and dynamic rules need different treatment. Retain snapshot audiences as labeled snapshots; recreate purchase-window or loyalty rules against confirmed destination fields. Inspect members who should enter and leave after a controlled purchase or status change. Keep old and new definitions together so operators can explain membership differences without claiming the destination reproduced unavailable event history.</p>
        <h2>Proposed test: a store QR code that outlives migration</h2>
        <p>For a hypothetical footwear retailer, inspect the printed QR code at a checkout counter, the website signup and an old social profile link. Confirm where each currently resolves. Replace obsolete collection destinations and inspect source attribution, disclosure and resulting channel subscription status in controlled signups. This is a proposed collection audit, not a claim that a retailer acquired subscribers or that any configuration guarantees legal compliance.</p>
        <h2>Plan number changes with the migration team</h2>
        <p>Attentive’s guide includes SMS setup, subscriber certification and a representative-assisted migration message when moving to a new number. Confirm the current requirements and destination coverage with the account team. Test replies and opt-out handling with controlled recipients before promotional activation. Do not promise preservation of the old sender identity or treat email subscription as text permission.</p>
        <h2>Loyalty integration migration is a separate project</h2>
        <p>The documented move to the Yotpo-built Loyalty integration updates matching existing Attentive subscriber profiles and requires new journeys and segments. Record which attributes and triggers each loyalty message uses. Inspect a matching member and a member without a matching subscriber profile. Do not assume installing the integration subscribes every loyalty member or makes legacy journeys safe to leave enabled.</p>
        <h2>Proposed test: loyalty reminder beside review collection</h2>
        <p>Create a controlled eligible member with a recent purchase and a pending review request. Inspect which app schedules each message and whether the loyalty change reaches the intended profile. Review the old and new journeys for duplicate entry. The Loyalty migration guide says old journeys and segments remain in the interface; work with support on deprecated assets after validating replacements. This is an operating check, not a measured retention outcome.</p>
        <h2>Procurement should specify the actual program</h2>
        <p>Ask for a proposal covering email sends, SMS destinations and volume, sender setup, support, users and required approval controls. Demonstrate the actual approval and reporting path rather than assuming multi-brand governance from a vendor label. Compare low-activity and peak periods using the quote terms. Keep retained review and loyalty services in the budget; do not compare against a discontinued suite bundle.</p>
        <h2>Choose email ownership explicitly</h2>
        <p>Attentive’s migration documentation covers email as well as SMS. Review its proposed welcome, recovery and post-purchase journeys before adding another sender. Sequenzy can be another email candidate if a specific requirement is unmet, but a split stack needs named owners for subscriptions and overlapping flows. Choose based on the demonstrated program and operating responsibility rather than unsupported enterprise or DTC rankings.</p>
        <h2>Sources and limits</h2>
        <p>Supplier documentation checked November 24, 2026. These are documented scope and proposed acceptance checks, not firsthand product tests. Account access, current quotes and live integration payloads remain unverified.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><a class="link" href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">Yotpo end-of-life notice</a></li>
          <li><a class="link" href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">Yotpo named partners</a></li>
          <li><a class="link" href="https://help.attentive.com/hc/en-us/articles/41028229226900-Migrate-your-email-and-SMS-program-from-Yotpo-to-Attentive">Attentive Yotpo migration guide</a></li>
          <li><a class="link" href="https://help.attentive.com/hc/en-us/articles/42756279288468-Yotpo-Loyalty-Integration-Migration">Attentive Loyalty integration migration</a></li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-white">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/postscript-vs-attentive">Postscript vs Attentive</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/yotpo-email-sms-vs-postscript">Yotpo vs Postscript</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-attentive">Omnisend vs Attentive</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/fashion">Email apps for fashion stores</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Yotpo Email & SMS vs Attentive FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
